  },
  {
   "id": "lsm_t",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 888,
   "y": 1024,
   "width": 784,
   "height": 82,
   "strokeColor": "#ffffff",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100053,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700371,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "write path - append-only, sequential:\n  1. new email -> tokenize -> posting entries in a memtable\n  2. memtable full -> flush SEQUENTIALLY to an immutable\n     on-disk sorted file\n  3. background: merge small files into larger ones\nread path: query the memtable + each on-disk level, merge",
   "originalText": "write path - append-only, sequential:\n  1. new email -> tokenize -> posting entries in a memtable\n  2. memtable full -> flush SEQUENTIALLY to an immutable\n     on-disk sorted file\n  3. background: merge small files into larger ones\nread path: query the memtable + each on-disk level, merge",
   "fontSize": 11,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "middle",
   "containerId": "lsm",
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "o2n",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 880,
   "y": 1136,
   "width": 532,
   "height": 225,
   "strokeColor": "#64748b",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100054,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700378,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "LSM's whole premise is that WRITES ARE SEQUENTIAL APPENDS AND READS PAY\nTHE COST - and sequential disk is ~244x faster than random, so an\ningest-dominated workload wants exactly this trade. Same structure as\nCassandra, RocksDB and BigTable, and the same structure the metadata\nstore already uses - so the index can live IN the metadata store,\nkeyed by user_id, eliminating a second system entirely. Tuned for one\nmailbox, with no merge contention across users.\n\nThe costs are large and mostly human: you are building a search\nengine. Tokenization, stemming, stop words, Unicode normalization, CJK\nsegmentation, phrase queries, ranking - each a well-understood problem\nwith a long tail of correctness bugs, and 80 languages means 80 tails.\nPlus read amplification across levels (mitigated by a Bloom filter per\nfile, which is exactly why LSM stores ship with them), and none of\nElasticsearch's decade of production hardening.",
   "originalText": "LSM's whole premise is that WRITES ARE SEQUENTIAL APPENDS AND READS PAY\nTHE COST - and sequential disk is ~244x faster than random, so an\ningest-dominated workload wants exactly this trade. Same structure as\nCassandra, RocksDB and BigTable, and the same structure the metadata\nstore already uses - so the index can live IN the metadata store,\nkeyed by user_id, eliminating a second system entirely. Tuned for one\nmailbox, with no merge contention across users.\n\nThe costs are large and mostly human: you are building a search\nengine. Tokenization, stemming, stop words, Unicode normalization, CJK\nsegmentation, phrase queries, ranking - each a well-understood problem\nwith a long tail of correctness bugs, and 80 languages means 80 tails.\nPlus read amplification across levels (mitigated by a Bloom filter per\nfile, which is exactly why LSM stores ship with them), and none of\nElasticsearch's decade of production hardening.",
   "fontSize": 12,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "pick",
   "type": "rectangle",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 1364,
   "width": 1650,
   "height": 44,
   "strokeColor": "#047857",
   "backgroundColor": "#a7f3d0",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100055,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700385,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": [
    {
     "id": "pick_t",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "roundness": {
    "type": 3
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "pick_t",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 38,
   "y": 1377,
   "width": 1634,
   "height": 17,
   "strokeColor": "#374151",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100056,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700392,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "START with Elasticsearch and the mutable-field reduction.   SWITCH to a custom LSM index when merge pressure is MEASURABLY the binding constraint.",
   "originalText": "START with Elasticsearch and the mutable-field reduction.   SWITCH to a custom LSM index when merge pressure is MEASURABLY the binding constraint.",
   "fontSize": 14,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "center",
   "verticalAlign": "middle",
   "containerId": "pick",
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "pn",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 30,
   "y": 1424,
   "width": 1003,
   "height": 81,
   "strokeColor": "#64748b",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 1,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "seed": 100057,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 700399,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "groupIds": [],
   "boundElements": null,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "With the reduction done, Elasticsearch's remaining weakness is merge pressure at 10 billion writes/day - demanding but not\nobviously infeasible, and mitigated by per-user sharding (merges stay local) and by tuning refresh intervals against the\nseconds-freshness requirement. At Gmail's scale the crossover point arrives, which is why Gmail does not run Elasticsearch.\nBelow it, building a multilingual search engine to avoid operating a cluster is a bad trade - years of engineering to save a\ncluster.",
   "originalText": "With the reduction done, Elasticsearch's remaining weakness is merge pressure at 10 billion writes/day - demanding but not\nobviously infeasible, and mitigated by per-user sharding (merges stay local) and by tuning refresh intervals against the\nseconds-freshness requirement. At Gmail's scale the crossover point arrives, which is why Gmail does not run Elasticsearch.\nBelow it, building a multilingual search engine to avoid operating a cluster is a bad trade - years of engineering to save a\ncluster.",
   "fontSize": 13,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "d4",
